WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.112
Designing schemas that extract well.

2
00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:04.931
A schema is a prompt as much as a contract.

3
00:00:05.211 --> 00:00:07.472
Five rules cover most of what goes wrong.

4
00:00:07.752 --> 00:00:09.181
Describe every field.

5
00:00:09.461 --> 00:00:11.787
Descriptions go to the model with the schema.

6
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:22.605
Say what belongs in the field, in what form, and what to do when the source is silent: “Person who committed to the task, as written in the transcript; null if no one was named.

7
00:00:22.885 --> 00:00:23.183
”.

8
00:00:23.463 --> 00:00:24.850
Enumerate closed sets.

9
00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.567
Priority, status, category: if your code branches on it, make it an enum.

10
00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:33.452
Free strings drift (“High”, “high priority”, “P1”).

11
00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:35.162
Make “not found” legal.

12
00:00:35.442 --> 00:00:40.711
Every field the source might not cover should be nullable, or have an explicit “unknown” value.

13
00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:43.935
Otherwise constrained decoding forces an invention.

14
00:00:44.215 --> 00:00:51.980
Required-but-nullable, from lesson 7, is the usual pattern: the model must address the field, and null is a valid answer.

15
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.284
Keep it shallow.

16
00:00:53.564 --> 00:00:58.130
Nest only where the data is really nested (a list of items, each with fields).

17
00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:10.356
Deep trees of small objects are harder to fill correctly, and providers impose complexity limits; Claude’s docs, for example, cap optional parameters and union-typed parameters per request.

18
00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:13.495
Keep the API schema to the supported subset.

19
00:01:13.775 --> 00:01:17.978
Close every object, and leave numeric and length bounds to your validator.

20
00:01:18.258 --> 00:01:24.572
The SDK does this for you when it starts from Pydantic, but hand-written schemas sent to output_config.

21
00:01:24.852 --> 00:01:27.711
format must follow the rules or the request fails.

22
00:01:27.991 --> 00:01:38.167
On model choice: structured outputs are available across current Claude tiers (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku), so the guarantee doesn’t force you onto the largest model.

23
00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:49.284
Whether a smaller, cheaper model gets the values right as often is an empirical question for your eval set; the project’s optional stretch goal asks you to measure exactly that.
